              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Prove that twice the sum of two squares is the sum of two squares. Here is an example:",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p9-q16-a-example-native",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "center",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "2(5^2+3^2)=2(25+9)=68=64+4=8^2+2^2",
                  "display": true
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p9-q16-a-hint-native",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Hint: expand ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "(a+b)^2+(a-b)^2",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2(a^2+b^2)=(a+b)^2+(a-b)^2$.\n\nExpand the two squares; their cross terms cancel.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(a+b)^2+(a-b)^2&=a^2+2ab+b^2+a^2-2ab+b^2\\\\(a+b)^2+(a-b)^2&=2a^2+2b^2\\\\(a+b)^2+(a-b)^2&=2(a^2+b^2).\\end{align*}$$\nThus twice the sum of the squares of $a$ and $b$ is the sum of the squares of $a+b$ and $a-b$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p9-q16-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Prove that three times the sum of three squares is the sum of four squares.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$3(a^2+b^2+c^2)=(a-b)^2+(a-c)^2+(b-c)^2+(a+b+c)^2$.\n\nThe cross terms cancel when the four squares are expanded.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}3(a^2+b^2+c^2)&=(a^2+b^2)+(a^2+c^2)+(b^2+c^2)+(a^2+b^2+c^2)\\\\3(a^2+b^2+c^2)&=(a^2-2ab+b^2)+(a^2-2ac+c^2)\\\\&\\quad +(b^2-2bc+c^2)+(a^2+b^2+c^2+2ab+2ac+2bc)\\\\3(a^2+b^2+c^2)&=(a-b)^2+(a-c)^2+(b-c)^2+(a+b+c)^2.\\end{align*}$$\nThe right-hand side is the sum of four squares.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 58
      }
    ]
